<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The AdMob identifiers, in one file so that going live is one edit and not a search.

    These are the live identifiers of the Ravel app in AdMob, and they were Google's public test
    ones until this commit. That change is the point at which every ad request became real
    inventory: an impression counts, a click counts, and a click made by the person who wrote the
    app counts as invalid traffic. Nothing in the code can tell the difference - see
    ad_test_devices below, which is the only thing standing between a developer testing the reward
    path and a suspended account.

    The application id also has to reach the manifest, which cannot read a Kotlin constant, so both
    live as resources rather than one as a resource and one as a constant. They are marked
    untranslatable because they are identifiers, not text.

    They belong together and are replaced together: the application id names the AdMob app, the unit
    id names one ad placement inside it, and a mismatched pair - one real, one test - is a
    configuration AdMob rejects at request time rather than at build time. Both carry the same
    publisher number, which is the quick way to see they are from the same account.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="admob_application_id" translatable="false">ca-app-pub-3410450408196791~4964715314</string>
    <string name="ad_unit_rewarded" translatable="false">ca-app-pub-3410450408196791/8712388633</string>

    <!--
        Devices that must always be served test ads, by their hashed advertising id.

        The identifiers above are live, so this list is now the only protection there is. An ad
        request from your own phone is a real impression, and tapping it is a click on your own
        inventory - which AdMob treats as invalid traffic and answers by suspending the account, not
        by discounting the click. A developer testing a rewarded ad taps it every time, because
        tapping is how you find out the reward arrives.

        **This list being empty means the next run of the app on your own device is unprotected.**
        Filling it is the first thing to do after the identifiers changed, not the last.

        The hash is not guessable and has to be read off the device: run the app, ask for an ad, and
        the SDK logs the exact line to paste, of the form
        "RequestConfiguration.Builder().setTestDeviceIds(Arrays.asList("33BE2250B43518CCDA7DE426D04EE231"))".
        It changes when the advertising id is reset.

        A hash that is not a real one matches no device, which looks identical to having configured
        nothing - so an entry here is only worth as much as the log line it was copied from. Add
        every device the app is developed on, not only the one being used today.

        Not limited to debug builds on purpose. Naming a device here only ever costs that one device
        its real ads, and the alternative - a list that stops working in a release build - is a
        developer running a signed build against live inventory with no protection at all.
    -->
    <string-array name="ad_test_devices" translatable="false">
        <item>62E9F968EA197E9A42C783903F5A6085</item>
    </string-array>
</resources>
